title: 'eth-rpc: keep the sync head behind un-indexed subscription gaps'
doc:
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    The subscription gap filler no longer lets the `sync-head` label advance over blocks it failed to index. Previously a gap fill that errored, or a gap dropped because the gap queue was full, still counted as done, so the head moved past the missing blocks and a restart never filled them. `eth_getLogs` over a block range then omitted those blocks' logs, `eth_getLogs` by such a block's Ethereum block hash reported the block as unknown, and the lookups served from the index returned `null`, `0` or an error for them, for example `eth_getTransactionReceipt`, `eth_getTransactionByHash`, `eth_getTransactionByBlockHashAndIndex`, `eth_getTransactionByBlockNumberAndIndex`, `eth_getBlockTransactionCountByHash`, `eth_getBlockTransactionCountByNumber`, `debug_traceTransaction`, `polkadot_postDispatchWeight`, and every method addressed by such a block's Ethereum block hash, whose mapping to the substrate block was never written. Methods that read the node, such as `eth_getBlockReceipts` by number and `eth_getTransactionCount`, were not affected. A finalized block whose own processing failed was skipped the same way; it is now handed to the gap filler like a missed block. A block whose database write failed stayed marked as indexed in memory, so when the write failed while it was the best block, the finalized stream skipped it; the mark is now dropped when the write fails, so the finalized stream processes the block again. This applies in archive mode and with `--eth-pruning <N>`. Blocks finalized between the start of the backward sync and the start of the finalized subscription could stay unindexed the same way; they are now queued like a missed range. A block the gap filler indexes does not evict a retracted sibling at the same height that was indexed while it was the best block; both stay in the index until a resync. Until then `eth_getLogs` over a block range containing that height returns the logs of both blocks, and a transaction that exists only in the retracted block keeps resolving, for example through `eth_getTransactionReceipt`. Such a block is also missing from `eth_feeHistory`, and its logs are not pushed to `eth_subscribe` log subscriptions.

    A failed gap fill is now attempted up to 5 times, waiting 2, 4, 8 and then 16 seconds between attempts, about 30 seconds of waiting plus the attempts themselves, each of which walks the range again; an outage that outlasts them leaves the gap to the restart described below. In archive mode (`--eth-pruning=archive`) a gap that still fails, or that did not fit in the queue, keeps the sync head at its current block for the rest of the process lifetime. Later blocks are still indexed and served, but the index-backed methods listed above stay incomplete for the gap's blocks until the next restart, whose backward sync walks from the current finalized block down to the block after the frozen head and extracts the blocks that are not yet indexed. That walk still fetches every block's header and EVM block hash from the node, so it takes longer the longer the head has been frozen, and it records its progress only at the end: a restart during the walk starts it over. The walk stops at the first block it cannot index and revisits nothing below that block until a later restart gets past it. The historic sync then does not complete, so the sync head stays frozen for the whole process even once no gap fill is pending, and a block that fails on every restart keeps it frozen, with every gap below that block unfilled, until its cause is fixed or the database is resynced. Detection is log-only: `/health` does not reflect a frozen sync head. Operators who see the error "in archive mode the sync head stays at its current block until a restart fills the gap" should restart eth-rpc. A restart fills a gap only once its cause, and the cause of every failing block above it, has cleared. When the restart cannot sync the gap, it logs the failing block at once with a line naming it (`eth_block_hash failed for`, `Insert failed for`, `Could not fetch start block`, `Could not fetch header of` or `Could not fetch parent of`), followed by `Sync of new blocks ... stopped due to`, and the sync head stays at its block; a backfill of older history that is still running continues. The summary line `Sync stopped due to` appears only once that backfill has also ended. When the connected node no longer has the gap's state, point eth-rpc at an archive node and restart again; deleting the database and resyncing only helps with such a node. When the same block fails on every restart against an archive node, look for the cause in the `attempt N failed` warning and the per-block line above, in the node's logs for that block, in whether the node's response for it exceeds `--rpc-max-response-size`, and in the database (disk space, permissions, corruption); report the block to the eth-rpc maintainers only if none of these explains it. With `--eth-pruning <N>` there is no sync head and the index is in memory, so a restart does not help: the gap's blocks stay unindexed until they leave the retained window. Handing a finalized block to the gap filler because its processing failed, and queuing the blocks between the backward sync's start and the subscription's start, apply to archive mode only; with `--eth-pruning <N>` those two kinds of blocks are logged or left unindexed, as before.

    The fix prevents new holes only. An archive database that already lost blocks under the previous behaviour has the hole below its sync head, and nothing revisits it. The database keeps no record of such a hole, and the logs of earlier versions do not reliably show one: blocks finalized between the start of the backward sync and the start of the finalized subscription were skipped without any log line, and `Failed to process block`, which does not name its stream in those versions, marks a hole on either stream when the cause was a database write. `Subscription gap fill failed for` and `Subscription gap queue error, dropping` mark one as well. `Failed to resolve streamed` for a finalized block marks one only when it is logged for the first finalized block after a start: a later unresolved block was queued with the next resolved one, and a failed fill of it has its own line. To repair an archive database, delete it and restart eth-rpc against an archive node so that it resyncs, or check it against the chain first: a block whose Ethereum block hash, as returned by `eth_getBlockByNumber`, is not found by `eth_getBlockByHash` is missing from the index. No configuration changes are needed.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: patch
